=========================================================
PERCENTAGE (%) UNITS IN CSS
=========================================================

Definition:
Percentage (%) is a relative unit in CSS.
It ALWAYS depends on another value.

The percentage value is calculated based on a reference
value such as:
- Parent element
- Containing block
- Root element
- Specific CSS property rules

So, percentage values are NOT fixed.

---------------------------------------------------------
IMPORTANT RULE
---------------------------------------------------------

Percentage does not have a single fixed meaning.
Its behavior changes depending on the CSS property
being used.

---------------------------------------------------------
HOW PERCENTAGE (%) WORKS
---------------------------------------------------------

- A percentage is calculated relative to another value
- That reference value changes based on the property
- Because of this, % can behave differently in different
  situations

---------------------------------------------------------
PERCENTAGE (%) FOR FONT SIZE
---------------------------------------------------------

Reference:
Font size percentage is calculated based on the
PARENT element’s font size.

Example meaning (no code):
- Parent font size = 16px
- Child font size = 150%
- Resulting font size = 24px

Key Points:
- % inherits and multiplies parent font size
- Deep nesting can increase or decrease font size
- Can become hard to manage in large layouts

---------------------------------------------------------
PERCENTAGE (%) FOR WIDTH
---------------------------------------------------------

Reference:
Width percentage is calculated based on the
PARENT element’s width.

Example meaning:
- Parent width = 1000px
- Child width = 50%
- Resulting width = 500px

Key Points:
- Very useful for responsive layouts
- Automatically adjusts when parent resizes
- Commonly used in fluid layouts

---------------------------------------------------------
PERCENTAGE (%) FOR HEIGHT
---------------------------------------------------------

Reference:
Height percentage is calculated based on the
PARENT element’s height.

Important Rule:
Percentage height works ONLY if the parent has a
defined height.

If the parent height is not set:
- Percentage height will NOT work as expected

---------------------------------------------------------
PERCENTAGE (%) FOR PADDING
---------------------------------------------------------

Important Behavior:
Padding percentages are calculated based on the
PARENT element’s WIDTH, not height.

This applies to:
- padding-top
- padding-bottom
- padding-left
- padding-right

Use Case:
This behavior is commonly used for maintaining
aspect ratios (like responsive images or videos).

---------------------------------------------------------
PERCENTAGE (%) FOR MARGIN
---------------------------------------------------------

Reference:
Margin percentages are calculated based on the
PARENT element’s WIDTH.

Applies to:
- margin-top
- margin-bottom
- margin-left
- margin-right

Important Note:
Even vertical margins use parent width, not height.

---------------------------------------------------------
PERCENTAGE (%) FOR POSITIONING
---------------------------------------------------------

When used with:
- top
- right
- bottom
- left

Reference:
Percentage is calculated based on the size of the
containing block (usually the parent element).

---------------------------------------------------------
ADVANTAGES OF PERCENTAGE (%)
---------------------------------------------------------

- Responsive by nature
- Adapts automatically to parent size
- Useful for flexible layouts
- Helps reduce media queries

---------------------------------------------------------
DISADVANTAGES OF PERCENTAGE (%)
---------------------------------------------------------

- Can be confusing due to different reference rules
- Harder to predict in complex nested layouts
- Not ideal for precise fixed sizing

---------------------------------------------------------
WHEN TO USE PERCENTAGE (%)
---------------------------------------------------------

Use percentage when:
- Creating fluid layouts
- Working with widths and spacing
- Building responsive components

Avoid percentage when:
- Exact fixed size is required
- Deep nesting causes confusion
- Consistency across all screens is critical

---------------------------------------------------------
IMPORTANT NOTE
---------------------------------------------------------

Always remember:
Percentage (%) depends on context.
Understanding what it is relative to is key to
using it correctly.
